#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0dc3cc-1fcc-5234-969d-d0c45c4086d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f0dc3cc-1fcc-5234-969d-d0c45c4086d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291616} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe7058e-14c1-530a-b327-daaa9a9dd3a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b3e3683-194f-54fa-bd4c-fab6903bc098" :question/math-academy-id "q-327713" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa2e911-c5aa-57d2-ac62-d3b4d243113b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291619} {:answer/id #uuid "f92fa46d-b9a4-5869-b341-90d7376697fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92fa46d-b9a4-5869-b341-90d7376697fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291620} :answer-field/id #uuid "95567c78-423d-51cf-921f-00d164d442d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88777a8c-b856-53ff-a931-cd87ef035449" :question/math-academy-id "q-327714" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4147cf9-c8f1-5144-97b4-cfa094e08775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4147cf9-c8f1-5144-97b4-cfa094e08775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291623} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b0c08a-9e12-5e55-ae11-de28cb01100b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd3bfe1-d629-5d9d-a3c1-fae9fceec25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fd3bfe1-d629-5d9d-a3c1-fae9fceec25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291625} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfa513d-30dd-5dc0-984e-f53a0eadea58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5112b0c9-2348-5c77-aec1-76fb30e4dd13" :question/math-academy-id "q-327990" :question/problem "Express $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+27 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =36 \\\\ R & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+{60}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0e4f69-f187-596a-93fe-0a45e2a885bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0e4f69-f187-596a-93fe-0a45e2a885bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291628} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34f41ac-df72-5121-b1e8-06d32f5088f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0097a2f1-0dce-51d6-9750-e9244aad6045" :question/math-academy-id "q-327996" :question/problem "Given that the expression $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74838ab7-63a4-562d-a605-d85059a311a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291631} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b2c173-e5e0-5eb1-b118-59165e114b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291632} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f14912-6504-5499-ac89-a4bfb6773fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291633} {:answer/id #uuid "01791274-6d7f-511b-b580-5ec19b37ea8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291634} {:answer/id #uuid "c945af91-91f3-5783-a7e6-564f9bfa4e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74838ab7-63a4-562d-a605-d85059a311a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291631} :answer-field/id #uuid "7119c7f7-91a1-5980-92f5-9960ac6b2ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f08f64f5-5b73-5bda-84f5-7bef240adac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65508" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$"}]}
